/** siman 467 batch A — chunk 6: beur-hagra, chok-yaakov */
export const CHUNK6 = {
  "beur-hagra:1:א":
    "Seif 1 — just as, etc. Since Rava needed to sell it:",
  "beur-hagra:1:ב":
    "Rather he sells it. The Rambam's version: keva keva so that, etc.; and there is an alternate version \"to an Israelite,\" and it refers to what was said initially to sell to Eretz Yisrael; but to an Israelite, since he informs him, it is permitted in any amount, as written in chapter 3 of Demai mishnah 3 R' Yehuda certainly permits, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra:2:א":
    "Seif 2 — this grain. Therefore Rava did not ask whether they split open. Hagahot Maimoni and Ra'avan there:",
  "beur-hagra:2:ב": "From the force of a double uncertainty. As written in Kesubos 9a:",
  "beur-hagra:2:ג":
    "But not to eat it. Since he has permissible grain, a double uncertainty does not help, as written at the beginning of Yoma 3b; and its uncertainty is forbidden, and if it became mixed, etc. Mordechai end of chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah, and see Yoreh De'ah siman 110.",
  "beur-hagra:3:_":
    "Seif 3 — wheat, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc. As written in chapter 3 of Gittin: one who lends money, etc., even though death is common, as written, and in Sukkah 24a:",
  "beur-hagra:4:_":
    "Seif 4 — a year, etc., for presumptive prohibition, and we follow the majority, as written in Gittin there: one who brings a get and left it, etc., and there in the Gemara that most sick people [recover] to life:",
  "beur-hagra:5:_":
    "Seif 5 — grain, etc. As written in chapter Kol Sha'ah that it leavens while attached, and therefore in such a case:",
  "beur-hagra:6:_":
    "Seif 6 — wheat, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc., as above, and as written in Gittin there: one who leaves produce, etc., if they were lost, etc., and the conclusion there in the Gemara from the outset of inspection, and Rashba there s.v. mial'el, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra:7:_":
    "Seif 7 — an Israelite, etc., for it is like chametz upon which rubble fell, as written in chapter Kol Sha'ah, and as Rashi wrote in chapter 1 regarding one who makes his house a storehouse; and further that there is a double uncertainty perhaps they did not leaven, and if you say they leavened, perhaps they spoiled — therefore it is permitted to retain, as written in seif 2. Rashba:",
  "beur-hagra:8:א":
    "Seif 8 — honey, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc., as above; and further that even if flour became mixed in it there is no prohibition, as the Rosh wrote on the Yerushalmi mentioned above R' Chananel, etc., it would be forged honey with flour, etc., and the concern is distant that perhaps water became mixed in the honey:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ב":
    "However there are those who are stringent. That they are accustomed to mix flour into it for sweetness, and therefore the Yerushalmi said one must burn because of the honey, not like the Rosh, and as Tosafos wrote in chapter Kol Sha'ah and in Menachos on red vinegar because they leaven barley in water first and we establish presumptive prohibition in such a case because they profit from forgery, as written in Avodah Zarah 34b — that cellar of morayis, etc., lest because he sealed it the wine prices equalize, etc., and see there in Tosafos 32a s.v. mai'otzar. But if he did not see him drawing, etc., and so too here since they profit from it:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ג":
    "And figs, etc., hanging, etc. — that figs are accustomed to mix flour into them for sweetness; and grapes are accustomed to dry them in an oven near bread or after they removed the bread from the oven; and in places where they do not mix flour and dry in the sun it is permitted — see in Tosafos Avodah Zarah there s.v. hai chala, etc., therefore, etc., since there are places, etc., and so is implied in the Gemara hai chala, etc., that they mix — meaning they are accustomed to mix — it implies only in places where they are accustomed:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ד": "And therefore they were accustomed. Like grapes as above:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ה": "And tsuker. That they mix flour into it:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ו": "But on Yom Tov. However on other days, even those who do not mix, it is forbidden because of confusion:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ז":
    "And the custom. That they forge with flour to establish its appearance, and so they place on se'or to restore its appearance:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ח": "negeliach. For they soak it in barley water:",
  "beur-hagra:8:ט":
    "However. For regarding rabbinic any-amount we do not establish presumptive prohibition b'dieved:",
  "beur-hagra:9:א":
    "Seif 9 — cooked dish. In chapter Kol Sha'ah 40a — taught: one may not soak, etc., R' Chananel: they did not split open, etc., and Shmuel said, etc., and we conclude in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah regarding Torah law we follow the stringent and regarding scribal law we follow the lenient — and so too here since mixtures of any amount is rabbinic:",
  "beur-hagra:9:ב":
    "Or wheat. To exclude from Ra'em who forbids wheat per his words even if they did not split open at all since they have splitting, and so wrote Eliyah Rabbah there s.v. ve'yeish omrim, etc., and he brings proof from the latter chapter of Avodah Zarah 65b — but it is not so, for the conclusion there of Kol Sha'ah is that wheat is lighter than barley, and that case in Avodah Zarah is not relevant here; and further he brings proof from that cellar steeped in chaff and they did not require there split open, and the Ran wrote that is not proof since there they were split open — and see below seif 2; and also one should not read that wheat even if split open is permitted, for if so the guarding below is unnecessary. But in Tosefta chapter 3 it implies a distinction between wheat and barley, for it teaches there: one soaks wheat and barley in water and they leavened — forbidden; R' Yose says: one who soaks barley in water and they split open — forbidden, and in vinegar, etc., and it teaches it in the name of R' Yose from the latter part — and in vinegar, etc., for he stated it as written in the Gemara:",
  "beur-hagra:9:ג":
    "The cooked dish is permitted. For in rabbinic law we follow leniency, as written in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, even though its essence is from Torah law as written in chapter 5 that we say it is different since it does not exist as in normal circumstances — even though its essence is from Torah — Ran:",
  "beur-hagra:9:ד":
    "And the custom is not, etc. That they conduct like Mar Ukva and we are not expert in the measure fit for splitting. Semag and Mordechai. And it appears to me the reason for the custom as written in seif 2 this grain, etc., and the Rif holds that even in such a case we require split open, and in the incident of the cellar as the Ran wrote that there they were split open as above, and so in Tosefta one soaks barley, etc., as above:",
  "beur-hagra:10:א":
    "Seif 10 — chicken, etc. As written in Avodah Zarah 65b — that crate of wheat, etc., and Mordechai chapter 2 and so too Ramban there from Yerushalmi chapter 3 of Orlah on mishnah: a garment dyed with orlah peels — it must burn; and we learned: if one removed from it a saddlecloth and wove the garment — forbidden in benefit; and we learned: if yayin nesech fell into a pit — all is forbidden in benefit; and we learned Rabbenu Shimon says: sell all of it to a gentile except the value of the yayin nesech in it — R' Yirmiyah bar Acha questioned: who is like that? There gentiles are not accustomed to buy wine from gentiles, but here gentiles are accustomed to buy garments from an Israelite — therefore anything that is not customary to buy from a gentile is permitted in all prohibitions, and so is the opinion of the Rosh in Orlah there, and Tosafos Zevachim 72b s.v. kulam and Yevamos chapter 1 s.v. kulam, etc., except that Mordechai challenged that the drop around the wheat kernel is chametz and it is like wine in wine that is permitted only for yayin nesech of rabbis and not d'oraisa as written above; but in Milchamot there he wrote that other prohibitions even if their prohibition is d'oraisa nevertheless for stam yeinam we compare them, for we are more stringent with stam yeinam than all prohibitions since it is in any amount, and we say in chapter 2 of Pesachim and chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah, etc., that this d'oraisa, etc., whatever the rabbis enacted, etc., and specifically complete yayin nesech which is among prohibitions atop prohibitions we are stringent, etc., and so wrote the Ran in Avodah Zarah there — except that he wrote regarding redemption that even in stam yeinam specifically barrel in barrel and not wine in wine — see what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 142:",
  "beur-hagra:10:ב":
    "That it is not sold, etc. — meaning one need not say except the chametz, etc., and as in that cellar of wheat in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah that Rava permitted to sell them, etc., anonymously — Mordechai there siman 491 in the name of R' Yechiel of Paris — see there, and Tosafos Chullin 99 s.v. vehalachta, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra:10:ג":
    "And in these countries, etc. — for they hold specifically in stam yeinam the rabbis were lenient, as written above in Yoreh De'ah letter 1 practical halachah, etc., and see Mordechai double uncertainty 491 and siman 493, and even though in other matters we do not distinguish between stam yeinam and chametz as written there 34a and chapter Kol Sha'ah, etc., whatever the rabbis enacted, etc., here the Gemara distinguishes explicitly:",
  "beur-hagra:10:ד":
    "And if it was found on the eighth day, etc. — Magen Avraham, and the same in seif 9 in a cooked dish wheat and barley were found:",
  "beur-hagra:11:א":
    "Seif 11 — if it was found, etc. — for if it is not soaked, per his words it is permitted, for presumably it was not in water since they are guarded; and if it was in flour or dough it does not hurry to leaven more than the dough — soaked is not chametz but only hurries to leaven from what is written there: one soaks in vinegar, etc. Rosh there:",
  "beur-hagra:11:ב":
    "One removes, etc. As written in chapter Keitzad Tzolin 97b: one takes its place — a drop from its gravy on the flour, one scoops, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra:11:ג":
    "And there is one, etc. — Magen Avraham. And further that the water in the dough, etc., and according to what the Rosh wrote regarding a se'or house that kneading is like hot with gravy that forbids in all of it, as written in Chullin 96b and 108a:2, and all the more so in matzah that was baked:",
  "beur-hagra:11:ד": "And they permit, etc., and we say. As written in Kesubos 75b:",
  "beur-hagra:11:ה":
    "And if it was found — specifically from place to place we say here it was found, but not from time to time as written there while still in her father's house we do not say it was born now, and in Niddah 3:2 one who wrings out, etc., — he did not say except, etc., even though from place to place they argue — Chizkiyah and R' Yochanan, for R' Yehuda holds in impurity we are stringent — see Tosafos there 4a s.v. ve'mai. And one can say Chizkiyah holds that even in impurity we do not establish presumptive prohibition, and nevertheless Chizkiyah concedes to R' Mona as written there — and Magen Avraham note 18:",
  "beur-hagra:11:ו":
    "But before. It is nullified in sixty, and even though according to the first reason of Rashba that it touched here and here one should forbid even before Pesach — one need not be concerned for this since it is cold — see Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 in the gloss, and if he does not know, etc., and all the more so here; nevertheless for removal one needs even before Pesach, for we consider it chametz at the place of contact as above — only in its uncertainty we are not stringent:",
  "beur-hagra:12:א":
    "Seif 12 — if they scalded, etc. — for we do not say after they removed the chicken it fell, for we do not say from time to time as above in seif 11:",
  "beur-hagra:12:ב":
    "And if an embedded wheat kernel was found, etc., there is nothing in this. So wrote the Tur: since the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste, and even complete chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason, and Beit Yosef wrote — even though from the words of the Rosh in chapter Kol Sha'ah in that case in seif 11 that a wheat kernel was found in dough he wrote one should not be concerned perhaps the wheat kernel was in water — forty days — for if it was in water it forbids in cold — there it deals during Pesach in any amount, and here it deals before Pesach; and proof from what the Tur wrote that in cold water the wheat kernel does not impart taste, etc., and if during Pesach why mention imparting taste — it forbids in any amount; and therefore he said there is nothing in this because the reason that it does not absorb in cold is because it is any amount — therefore some forbid during Pesach, and their reason is that they hold as Ribba in Tosafos Pesachim 76a s.v. tania that both sixty and cold rinsing are required — only its prohibition is slight unlike during Pesach; and it deals even in less than sixty, not like Magen Avraham as above:",
  "beur-hagra:12:ג":
    "And there is one who permits. Meaning since any amount is rabbinic we are lenient in its uncertainty as written in Shabbos 34a and Eruvin 5b and other places, and as the Rif wrote in a cooked dish where a wheat kernel was found as above in seif 9 — even though Rama does not hold thus, he wrote this opinion here because in cold or b'eyna one relies on this opinion as written:",
  "beur-hagra:12:ד":
    "And there is one, etc. — meaning the double uncertainty that perhaps after they removed the chicken it fell, and if you say it was in the chicken, perhaps it was digested in the chicken as written in chapter 1 of Ahilot: we learned regarding digestion how long it remains in the intestines, etc., and in birds and fish for the time until it falls to fire and burns, and we derive prohibition from impurity as written at the end of Avodah Zarah as they said in purity, so in prohibition, and see siman 462 seif 9, and as the Rif wrote in chapter 3 of Pesachim we learn that anything that does not become impure with impurity of foods, etc.; but Tosafos Menachos 69a and the Rosh in Ahilot questioned from Bechoros 7b that a kosher fish that swallowed an impure fish is forbidden in eating, and answer in the name of R' Hai that one distinguishes between prohibition and impurity, as written in chapter 1 of Yevamos — therefore R' Yehuda [disagrees] only regarding, etc.; and they further answered that fish are hard, and as in Menachos that meat is soft, etc., that bones are hard — therefore they said there wheat in cattle dung, etc., for eating it becomes impure with impurity of foods, even for meal offerings only because of \"present it now\" — acceptably to your offering; and they further answered that perhaps that case in Bechoros is according to Ribba who said in Ahilot regarding birds and fish from the outset without waiting it deals. And Ra'avan wrote that even according to the first tanna perhaps the fish swallowed a moment before it was caught and did not wait the measure of digestion, and as in Chullin 91a, and Rashba there s.v. mi lo iyakvi, etc., and digestion is only from the living as in Tosefta there, and the Rosh brought it: a dog that ate nevelah meat, etc., if it died — if it did not wait in its intestines, etc., from the outset the house is impure, and in birds, etc., and therefore he said from the force of double uncertainty and according to Ra'avan:",
  "beur-hagra:12:ה":
    "And regarding practical halachah. The conclusion of Tosafos there and Rosh there that whether for prohibition or impurity in anything whole that is recognizable that it was not digested and not chewed in his mouth, such as whole wheat in cattle dung and whole fish — therefore he cited fish that swallowed while whole, and see there and forty days from Tosafos in conclusion they are not concerned for these resolutions — therefore he ruled here in cold or b'eyna as the first version that we are lenient in uncertainty of any amount since it is rabbinic, and in a broken wheat kernel in a chicken he cited as Tosafos' conclusion that there is digestion only that he was concerned for Ra'avan's uncertainty and permits through double uncertainty; but in whole or in meat, since there is only one uncertainty and it is in hot and b'eyna is not stringent per Tosafos' version and their conclusion as above:",
  "beur-hagra:13:א":
    "Seif 13 — day-old. Otherwise remove and it is permitted according to Shulchan Aruch in siman 467 seif 10. Beit Yosef:",
  "beur-hagra:13:ב":
    "During Pesach. Before Pesach it is nullified in sixty in the pit. Beit Yosef. And even though hag'alah does not help during Pesach as in siman 452 seif 1 in the gloss, and even according to Shulchan Aruch who permits remove — nevertheless for day-old it does not help, and if so as Magen Avraham in the name of Darkei Moshe that he hag'eled before the time of prohibition and they descended into the pit during Pesach — even though it is taste imparted by taste imparted, nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden as Yoreh De'ah siman 95 seif 2 in the gloss, apart from the matters being strained in themselves — one for Shulchan Aruch there who permits even l'chatchila, and even for those who forbid there it is only because of custom, and here from the law it is forbidden; and further even for those who forbid there in such a case it is three taste imparted by taste imparted that is permitted as Tosafos end of Avodah Zarah and Rosh chapter Kol Sha'ah and as written above siman 432 seif 1 — except they hold like those who say that whatever is boiling releases, boiling absorbs, does not absorb — and according to this even during Pesach one may hag'el in any case only that they should not rest from their boiling, and as written there:",
  "beur-hagra:13:ג": "Because. Magen Avraham:",
  "beur-hagra:14:א":
    "Seif 14 — if it was found, etc. Magen Avraham and as written in Pesachim 76a: cold into hot, etc., and see there:",
  "beur-hagra:14:ב":
    "And if after. Salting is like boiling, roasting is like boiling as in Chullin 97b, and peeling suffices as in Pesachim there — one peels its place, and even for the stringent in roasting — as much as removal; and he compared to a drop from gravy, etc., there in salting they agree peeling suffices — Rosh in Chullin there, and see Yoreh De'ah siman 91 and siman 105, and all the more so per his words they objected in Chullin 112a, but roasting requires peeling — boiling is discussed, not like Tosafos there 96b s.v. ad and Rashi Pesachim 76a that cold is discussed, and so in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 91 that in roasting boiling is meant, and if so even in roasting peeling suffices — see below siman 467 seif 1 and what is written:",
  "beur-hagra:14:ג":
    "And there are those who are stringent, etc. Since its prohibition is in any amount and specifically around it one is concerned for that any amount. Kol Bo in the name of Ra'avad, and he holds the peel forbids again, not like Tosafos Chullin 96 s.v. afilu except that in other prohibitions since the prohibition absorbed in the peel does not forbid because it spreads in its depth as written here — and there are views against it unlike here — and one asks: let it make its depth nevelah and forbid again as Rabbenu Tam wrote there 101 in s.v. shekadam. And Or Zarua, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra:14:ד":
    "And if they were salted, etc. — meaning and he does not know perhaps it touched all of them — Magen Avraham and as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 and if it is not known, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra:14:ה":
    "And there are those who are stringent, etc. Magen Avraham and as in Chullin 97a — salting is different because it bubbles and salting is like boiling like roasting, and even though taste does not pass from piece to piece without gravy as in Chullin 108a:2 and Tosafos there s.v. tipach, etc., here there is concern perhaps it touched all of them and forbids all of them in any amount:",
  "beur-hagra:14:ו":
    "The essential ruling to forbid, etc. — for by law it is not forbidden even for those who require sixty in salting because we are not expert — only in something to which greasiness applies, unlike chametz as in Yoreh De'ah there in the gloss — only because of stringency therefore they do not forbid in uncertainty all of them that perhaps it touched them, only the peel that is forbidden by law — Magen Avraham:",
  "beur-hagra:14:ז":
    "And if he wishes, etc. — for otherwise he permits in Shulchan Aruch above seif 11, and even for those who forbid there because its prohibition is d'oraisa, but for rabbinic prohibitions not, and all the more so here which is only a stringency in itself:",
  "beur-hagra:15:א":
    "Seif 15 — chicken, etc., one cuts, etc. As in Chullin 96b and Pesachim 76b, and as Ra'avad wrote in siman 14 that one deepens, etc., as written there:",
  "beur-hagra:15:ב":
    "And there are those who forbid, etc. — for in their days they did not turn the spit as in Chullin 111a, and Rashba there s.v. halachta, etc., and he holds turning the spit is like something that bubbles that forbids all of it as written 97a, but taste does not pass from piece to piece as written there 108 and Tosafos s.v. tipat:",
  "beur-hagra:15:ג":
    "And so there is, etc. — for he holds turning the spit is like shaking and covering that spreads through all the pieces as written 108a:2 — see there in Tosafos:",
  "beur-hagra:16:א": "Seif 16 — the bird is permitted. Taz and Magen Avraham:",
  "beur-hagra:16:ב":
    "And the wheat kernel, etc., not like, etc. — for one does not derive prohibition from impurity. Tur. Therefore there is no distinction between whole or broken, and Tosafos Bechoros 7b and Chullin 94 s.v. amar Rava — and he cited this version because all other resolutions are strained when they distinguish whole from broken — for then what is challenged there from bones perhaps they were whole — and even though they strained there in Tosafos and similarly Ra'avan's answer that for it to fall to fire is a slight matter, and so in Tosefta it said in birds immediately — only answer that one must be stringent like Ribba that in Torah law we follow the stringent as Tosafos Chullin there, and Hagahot Ashiri there since it is hard it is only rabbinic, and all the more so to forbid roasting as the Tur wrote — but there is no need to distinguish prohibition from impurity, for Tosafos too in conclusion in Menachos do not hold thus. And proof they said Chullin 116b but they soak, etc., and in the stomach of a kosher animal, etc., and the Rif there, and Tosafos who wished to distinguish prohibition from impurity follow their views that distinguish clear from thick, and the essence is as they wrote that they distinguish hard from soft, and as stated there in the Gemara explicitly — therefore fish is forbidden and all the more so wheat that is firm — and therefore it said there wheat, etc., for eating it becomes impure, etc.:",

  "chok-yaakov:1:א":
    "Or that fell upon them. And this is the language of the Ran in chapter Kol Sha'ah: even if they split open such that the leavening is recognizable in them, nevertheless it is forbidden to sell to a gentile, for we are concerned lest he grind them and afterward sell to an Israelite — end of his words. However the Tur wrote and the chametz is not recognizable in them — it implies at first glance that if the chametz is recognizable in them we are not concerned at all, and it is difficult, for there is still the concern of the Ran. And similarly sensed this difficulty on the Tur in Olat Shabbat — see there — and Magen Avraham answered and this is its language: we are not concerned lest he grind them and sell them to an Israelite since one does not buy flour from the market except in a time of distress as written in siman 453, unlike Olat Shabbat — end of his words. But in truth he did not correct anything with this answer, for that one does not buy except in a time of distress means specifically for matzah of the commandment as written above siman 453 note 15, and even for us who are accustomed to perform guarding for all the matzos — nevertheless when an Israelite buys that flour before Pesach and retains it in his house he transgresses \"it shall not be seen,\" since the chametz was recognizable in them — and this is plainly the opinion of the Ran. Therefore it appears to me the main intent of the Tur in what he wrote and the chametz is not recognizable is for amplification: even though the chametz is not recognizable and only a general concern of leavening is known in it (as the Tur wrote explicitly), nevertheless we are concerned for mishap in the matter of selling, and all the more so when chametz is known and recognizable with certainty we are concerned for mishap — and it is clear:",
  "chok-yaakov:1:ב":
    "Forbidden to retain it. If he transgressed and retained it and sowed it in the ground and growths sprouted, the growths are permitted as written above siman 453:",
  "chok-yaakov:1:ג":
    "And informs. So is the language of the Rambam, and the Tur challenged him, for since our Talmud's version is \"we sell it keva keva to an Israelite\" so that it finishes before Pesach, and since it is permitted only a small amount — if he informs, even much is also permitted — see in Beit Yosef and other Acharonim how they reconcile the words of the Rambam and the Tur, and through this they raised several laws; and in truth this question was already asked before the Rashba in responsum siman 254, and he answered that it deals with informing and there is no difficulty even for much as well, since an Israelite does not buy much since it is forbidden to retain it on Pesach (the opposite of my purpose — why should he be forced to sell to a gentile little by little near Pesach, for a gentile is found at all times in every place who will buy from him and return to him after Pesach as stated above siman 448 — see there), and as we say in Nedarim that an Israelite does not benefit from him — he buys for less and sells for more — only they do not listen to him; the same here — end of his words. It is found that the words of the Rambam are correct without doubt, and one need not corrupt the versions before us as the Maggid Mishneh wrote, and what astonished Beit Yosef and other Acharonim that escaped them the words of the Rashba; and so is the essence that when he informs, even much is permitted to sell to an Israelite, unlike the implication of some Acharonim; and even little by little, if he sells to an Israelite he must inform — and so agreed the Acharonim:",
  "chok-yaakov:1:ד":
    "So that it finishes before Pesach. Explanation: even if you say that also that small amount he will sell to an Israelite, nevertheless that small amount the Israelite will eat before Pesach; and according to this it implies b'eyna on the fifth hour in a matter where it is impossible that it finish before Pesach it is forbidden to sell to a gentile even little by little from concern lest he sell to an Israelite — and so is the implication of the language of the Talmud and Rambam and other poskim, even according to the Maggid Mishneh per his version, according to the Rambam in our Talmud, and so agreed Magen Avraham, unlike many, and so is the implication of the Mechaber here. Nevertheless it appears to me that if it leavened for an Israelite — much grain b'eyna and it is a matter where he cannot sell to a gentile that it finish before Pesach and there is great loss — it is permitted to sell to a gentile who knows it and recognizes him that he will not touch it at all but guard it for him until after Pesach, and he may say to him until you take, etc., as explained above siman 448 seif 3 and 4 — then there is nothing to be concerned for. And what Magen Avraham wrote that selling little by little is forbidden in other prohibitions only here regarding chametz, since there are many uncertainties they were lenient — his words are correct, and he aligned with this what is also written in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 46 note 34, unlike Shach and Taz there, also in Peri Chadash siman 84 note 17 he aligned with my opinion — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:1:ה":
    "And he sells it to an Israelite. And if the gentile said to the Israelite even a word after he sold him wheat that these wheats are for sweets — it is like what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 216 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold them and they left his possession; however if he is believed, etc., it is forbidden — end quote — Magen Avraham. It implies before he sold them, since it is in his possession he is believed — and this is not so, for since one can say he intended to enhance his merchandise he is not believed as stated in Yevamos page 22, and so in Yoreh De'ah siman 116, and Shach and Taz, and so I raised in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 59 note 7 — see there; and it is possible this is not enhancement of the merchandise, for on the contrary greater enhancement is to wheat if they are not yet for sweets and are clean in themselves — therefore he is believed if it is in his possession — uncertain:",
  "chok-yaakov:2:א":
    "This grain that was submerged in a river. And whether it is permitted to nullify this grain in majority or in sixty before Pesach, since it is still a time of permission — the Acharonim expanded on this, and see above siman 442 note 18 and siman 467 note 15 what emerges from their words for practical halachah:",
  "chok-yaakov:2:ב":
    "For since it rested at ease they come to leavening. So wrote the Rosh and Tur in the name of R' Efrayim, and according to what the Tur and Rosh ruled regarding all wheat for sweets — even though it did not rest at ease, for eating a wheat kernel they require it — even if they did not actually split open but are close to splitting, they are also forbidden, and as I wrote — if so, we need here the reason that it rested at ease, even if the wheat kernel did not soften, it is also forbidden; but for those poskim who ruled like Shmuel that we require actual split open while not for sweets — if so, perhaps where it rested at ease it is only stringent on one side and we require at least close to splitting, and otherwise they are permitted. And the Mechaber holds that for eating the wheat kernel in its own form it is an uncertainty of Torah law and to be stringent as explained in seif 9 — therefore he ruled here where it rested at ease, even if it did not soften and is not close to splitting, it is also forbidden like the Tur and Rosh. And this is why the Mechaber was precise and wrote here even though it did not split open it is forbidden anonymously, and did not write as below in seif 9 even if they did not actually split open — rather to show that even though it is not close to splitting, they are also forbidden here for eating the wheat kernel in its own form; but in what is found inside a cooked dish the Mechaber ruled below seif 9 that if they did not actually split open, even though they are close to splitting, it is also permitted, even though it rested at ease inside the cooked dish — nevertheless one can say since the cooked dish is boiling it is like absorbed and does not leaven according to the Tur siman 461 and 467 — see there — only that there is concern it was in water before boiling, therefore we are not so stringent, and see above siman 454 and see there, and this is the essence that in a cooked dish which is only a mixture of any amount rabbinic we are not so stringent as Magen Avraham wrote in note 12 — see there; and according to what the Rambam wrote in chapter 5 of Laws of Chametz it appears to me the reason is simple: since grain that was mixed or cooked and did not actually split open is not Torah chametz, and also any amount rabbinic we do not forbid two rabbinic laws — and similarly explained Hagahot Maimoni there the words of the Rambam; and in this way one can explain the words of the Rif to the Rambam without doubt, unlike Beit Yosef who strains greatly in the intent of the Rambam's words here; according to this reason this grain mixed species in species even in less than sixty is only rabbinic prohibition, and one may also be lenient according to the Mechaber, and I expanded on all this to reconcile the poskim and the Mechaber with correct reasons — but for practical halachah Rama already concludes in seif 9 in the gloss that they are accustomed to forbid in every matter — see there. And it is clear from the Mechaber's words in seif 1 and this seif that even if they did not split open it is forbidden to sell to a gentile before Pesach unless he sells little by little, unlike that which is in Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah who permits through double uncertainty — see Shach Yoreh De'ah end of siman 57:",
  "chok-yaakov:2:ג":
    "They come to leavening. And if it has not yet waited the measure of a mil, as Magen Avraham wrote — even if it occurred on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile before it leavens:",
  "chok-yaakov:2:ד":
    "But not to eat them on Pesach it is forbidden. Even if some of them were eaten before Pesach, one may not permit eating the rest as Bach concludes here, unlike Ravyah who specifically dry with dry and one of them was eaten, or in three mixtures one may permit eating two as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 — unlike moist with moist there is no permission in this, and so Magen Avraham; (and see above siman 453 where it was explained that flour in flour is called moist with moist) and so I also raised in my pamphlet of uncertainties note 19, unlike Shach — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:2:ה":
    "And a double uncertainty does not help. In truth in this seif and in several places it is explained that we rely on double uncertainty even in eating — see Taz and Shach who wrote the reason: since according to Mar Ukva even if they did not split open it is forbidden, it is only a double uncertainty of dispute and not in the reality of the matter — we do not lenient so much in such an uncertainty; and see in my pamphlet of uncertainties newly 2 note 2 where it was explained that on the contrary, uncertainty of dispute is better than uncertainty of the act itself; and also one cannot say that therefore the Rav was stringent in such an uncertainty because all are one uncertainty whether these wheats leavened from the water or not — I already rejected this reasoning too in pamphlet of uncertainties siman 120, and see there what the Rav wrote many reasons on this, and the essence appears to me like the conclusion of the Taz that here for this reason they were stringent because there is no loss in retaining it — but in truth in a place of loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy and the like one may permit even in eating:",
  "chok-yaakov:3:א":
    "Dry and hard. However if water came upon them one should forbid the wheat kernel itself even though it did not change at all, as written in note 7, and even in cordovan wheat as stated in Pesachim in the case of Rav Gidal — see there in Rashi:",
  "chok-yaakov:3:ב":
    "And their appearance did not change. But if their appearance changed or they are moist, it is forbidden even to retain them — Tur in the name of BeHag:",
  "chok-yaakov:4:_":
    "We are not concerned for ordinary wheat. And Knesset HaGedolah wrote in practical halachah that he permitted buying wheat for Pesach even though they were stored in pits, all that we did not see in them a change in appearance and they are not moist — see there where he expanded, and if there are among them wheats that sprouted — see above siman 453:",
  "chok-yaakov:6:_":
    "The upper part of the stack and its lower part. Rokeach wrote in siman 263 and this is its language: and if a little dew or water and snow on his grain, he scrapes those wheat kernels and takes what is beneath them as stated in chapter Kol Sha'ah in Yerushalmi — end of his words:",
  "chok-yaakov:7:_":
    "Split wheat. But if they are not split open one should not be stringent here at all, for even split open there is no complete leavening here, only spoilage and loss, as the Rashba wrote in responsum, and see above siman 476 and what is written, and siman 466 from the law of sweat of heat:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:א":
    "We do not establish prohibition on it to say it is forged. So is the language of the Tur, and it implies that if we know flour is mixed in it we must burn as stated explicitly in Yerushalmi end of chapter Kol Sha'ah, and one may not rely on the permission the Rosh wrote in the name of R' Yonah, and this is its language: and if we come to forbid the honey because of concern for mixture, that honey is forbidden all year, for they put nevelah meat into it and it transforms into honey — and R' Yonah wrote that even if they put nevelah meat into it, since it dissolved and became honey it is permitted, for we follow the present — end of his words. From this, according to his words, even if we know with certainty flour is mixed in it one may permit it; nevertheless one may not rely on this permission against the Yerushalmi who are stringent against R' Yonah's opinion, for the Rosh himself in chapter Keitzad Mevarchin doubts this view of R' Yonah, and also in Knesset HaGedolah he wrote one may not rely on this permission lest it returns and revives during Pesach — see there where he expanded; nevertheless it appears to me all this is if we know with certainty flour was mixed into it — but from the ordinary, even though they are accustomed to forbid eating them in their own form, nevertheless if it became mixed in a cooked dish one may permit b'dieved even in less than sixty, or even if we know flour was mixed into it it is permitted to retain in his house, or if it became mixed in a cooked dish if there is great loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, even in less than sixty one who relies on R' Yonah's words has not lost, and as also written in Ma'adanei Melech chapter Keitzad Mevarchin page 53b to uphold R' Yonah's words — see there, and so is implied in Knesset HaGedolah itself; and even though in Darkei Moshe it implies one should forbid the cooked dish b'dieved if there are not sixty against the honey, and Magen Avraham agreed with him in note 11 — it appears because he left R' Yonah's words in uncertainty, and see in his words siman 456 note 3, and the main difficulty is from that which we say in Bechoros page 16 that milk of an animal is a novelty, since blood congealed and became milk it should be forbidden — we learn we follow the initial state, unlike R' Yonah's opinion, etc. — and if because of this it does not apply, and from honey there is no difficulty, for there it is different with milk, for one could say there should be no permission for any milk since all milk was formed from the prohibition of blood in the initial prohibition, even though it changed in appearance it did not change to a permitted matter — therefore they say it is a novelty that the Torah innovated. Unlike honey where the honey itself is permitted — if so, if they placed a forbidden thing in it that returns and transforms to make honey, we follow the present when it became a permitted thing which is honey — and why from Torah should we forbid it like other honey, since its taste and appearance are equal and it changed to a permitted thing — and it is clear. It is found there is no contradiction at all from that case of milk to R' Yonah's words regarding honey; also regarding musk (meaning what they call in foreign language pezum) that he wrote there R' Yonah — one can strain and distinguish similarly, but from there in any case there is room for doubt as Magen Avraham himself in siman 456, and from honey he left in uncertainty there is room for doubt, and as written — examine:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ב":
    "Only that which they bring in barrels. The implication of the Rav's words is that even to eat it thus in its own form it is permitted in such a case, and so the Taz compelled from the Rav's language, unlike Magen Avraham who wrote the Rav's opinion is like some Acharonim that even honey brought in barrels is also forbidden to eat thus in its own form, only to make a drink from it before Pesach they permitted — and this does not fit well; and for practical halachah the Rav's relative concludes in his book that this law changes according to places and countries, and so I heard that honey of the land of Piham which is good and select and has no concern for forgery — but what is brought from other places such as the land of the Ger and Bruna or from Poland they are accustomed to forbid it except that which is brought in barrels while still with the comb on it — each place has its custom and its practice, and this is also the Rav's intent in what he wrote and the custom in these countries, and in Knesset HaGedolah he expanded greatly and was stringent regarding honey because he investigated in countries that they mix much flour and water into it until there are not sixty against the flour they mix into it — see note 16; and he also wrote there it became clear to him with certainty that when they extract honey from the comb it is impossible that they do not put a little water into it, and therefore one may not knead with it at all, for it is fruit juice with water — end of his words, and all this follows his view and other Acharonim as above siman 462; however according to what is written there that with a little water there is no concern for a house of concern if he performed guarding — yet we are not at all accustomed to rich matzah as explained several times:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ג":
    "And dried grapes. Magen Avraham wrote in the name of Maharil: if he wishes to retain the opening of the sambur he can retain with our grapes — end of his words:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ד":
    "Any dried fruits. But they are accustomed to make from them a drink — meaning they pour cold water on them and soak them until a drink is made, and so Magen Avraham testified that such is the custom in the community of Kalish; but one may not cook with them, and see there where he expanded on the reason for the custom:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ה":
    "In a manner where there is no concern for chametz. And we do not decree this because of that, and similarly with honey in Bechoros it is permitted and we do not decree, because these things are common to bring honey from the hive and similarly fruits that an Israelite dries in his house he is careful about leavening — unlike saffron which is not common to grow in his garden, therefore it is forbidden even what grew in his garden, as Levush wrote:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ו":
    "Even to drink it is forbidden. The Rav's relative wrote in his book: and I saw a question came before our elder the Gaon Maharash z\"l after Pesach that he retained hot tsuker, and he ruled to sell it to a gentile — end of his words. And Taz and Magen Avraham wrote there is no prohibition in hot tsuker, or what we call clear tsuker — it is permitted to retain in his house; nevertheless they were accustomed to sell it l'chatchila, and b'dieved it is permitted, and that this is the Rav's intent in the gloss tsuker kandil, not what we now call tsuker kandil, and all the more so types of night tsuker that they coat with types of fruits and spices which have complete leavening — such is the content of their words. But in truth even l'chatchila one should be careful regarding retaining these things; nevertheless if he transgressed and retained them, or mixtures, and there is great loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, it is possible one should not forbid, for behold in the Talmud itself it implies it is only prohibition l'chatchila, and he wrote and this is its language: and similar that the world is not accustomed to forbid — end of his words; also Maharil Laws of Pesach implies it is only a distant concern, and in responsum siman 25 he wrote and this is its language: and they are accustomed to be careful with tsuker if it is not a great need for healing, for merchants mix several types into it — end of his words; and the reason appears to me there is uncertainty and many concerns: perhaps it was not mixed in, and if you say it was mixed in, perhaps it was nullified before Pesach, and if you say it was not nullified, perhaps they did not mix flour from wheat for sweets, and fruit juice does not leaven, and if you say for sweets, perhaps it did not leaven through this soaking — nevertheless before the decisor rules he needs investigation by a sage how and what was done, for this matter changes according to place and time, and l'chatchila they are accustomed to be stringent in every matter; but what we now call tsuker kandil many common people are lenient and this error draws them from the Rav's language in the gloss, and the Acharonim already agreed it is an error in their hands. And see in Knesset HaGedolah that he wrote also tsuker that is very fine has concern for leavening, and also the drink made from tsuker called syrup or latverig — Ashkenazim are accustomed to be stringent, and see above siman 466 notes 1 and 2; and on the last Yom Tov they eat, etc. — see Maharil that it was an incident that was so, but one cannot prove there is a distinction between the last Yom Tov and the first:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ז":
    "That they call saffron. And see Yoreh De'ah note 114 from the law of saffron all year, and Magen Avraham wrote and Turei Zahav: the way is to soak it in beer and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it, and if so the same applies to saffron — end quote, and see above end of siman 461 from the law of vessels dyed with saffron that one may not use them on Pesach:",
  "chok-yaakov:8:ח":
    "However we do not forbid their mixtures. It implies even in less than sixty it is also permitted, and see in note 16 from the law of honey, and in tsuker too one should not forbid mixtures even in types of night tsuker b'eyna, and in such a case one may be lenient as written in note 28 — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:9:א":
    "And if they did not actually split open. Even if it softened until close to splitting it is also permitted, and see above note 7 regarding the matter in its reason — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:9:ב":
    "The cooked dish is permitted. Magen Avraham wrote and this is its language: Bach wrote in the name of Kol Bo that if three barleys were found in a cooked dish, there is a presumption there are many and perhaps they split open; and that which the Mechaber omitted in Shulchan Aruch is because we do not hold thus, for the Taz wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 84 seif 9 in the name of Maharshal that we do not say presumption except regarding worms that multiply from it, but regarding something that comes from outside we do not — end quote Magen Avraham. Behold what he wrote that therefore the Mechaber omitted because we do not hold thus is well aligned with halachah, for the Rif and Rosh and Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah in the name of BeHag and Agudah in the name of many Geonim wrote explicitly, and we do not forbid this cooked dish where two or three barleys are found that did not split open, and the same applies more — only there should be sixty in the cooked dish — see there; therefore the Mechaber omitted the opinion of Kol Bo in the name of Ra'avad who is alone against all these great ones. However what is supported from the Taz's words in the name of Maharshal — behold Maharshal's words on this are not compelled at all, and this distinction is not found in any posek, for anonymously we say three times is a presumption, even though it is something from outside, nevertheless this cooked dish is presumed that wheat or barley fell into it; and there is some proof for this from that which we say three [graves] — here is a presumption of burial, even though it is something from outside — see chapter 9 of Nazir and Rashi there; and that which the poskim I wrote hold to be lenient here — one can say since two or three were found and did not split open, we say even if there are more they also did not split open, and they were not so stringent in rabbinic any-amount prohibition — all this appears to me plainly the opinion of the poskim and the Mechaber, and for practical purposes according to the Rav's gloss there is no relevance in all this:",
  "chok-yaakov:9:ג":
    "Rather they forbid everything in any amount. Since the Rav did not write this gloss after what the Mechaber wrote, and those wheats or barleys they burn — he should have written burn everything as written nearby seif 10 in the gloss; rather it certainly appears the Rav's opinion where they did not actually split open is the opinion of all the great poskim to permit the cooked dish even in eating — if so, this stringency suffices to forbid in eating and not in benefit, and see beginning siman 467, and even in actually split open the opinion of many poskim is to sell to a gentile as the Mechaber's opinion in seif 10 — therefore in any case one should not be so stringent when it is not split open. And further the main reason for the custom to be stringent is per what Mordechai wrote in chapter Kol Sha'ah, and he is stringent because of she'ilamuta, and it was already explained above siman 467 that we hold chametz is not called she'ilamuta; also what Mordechai further wrote there and this is its language: and so in Semag one should be stringent since we are not expert in what is called fit to split open, and because of custom it does not forbid in uncertainty, and one who is lenient for Yom Tov joy has not lost — end of his words. It appears his intent is to rule like Mar Ukva that close to splitting is also forbidden in a cooked dish — but in truth the Rif and Rambam and BeHag and Rosh and Tur and Ra'avad and Agudah and Rokeach and Kol Bo all permit the cooked dish as long as they did not actually split open — in this one need not be expert, for all are expert in this to see if they actually split open or not; therefore it seems fit to rely on them and permit at least in benefit, and similarly Taz wrote in this end; however in Taz note 15 it implies forbidden in benefit, and in my humble opinion in a place of great loss and the like one may permit at least in benefit, and especially since Mordechai himself concluded one who is lenient for Yom Tov joy has not lost — it implies even in eating one may permit; but Bach wrote that nowadays even for Yom Tov joy they are not accustomed to be lenient, and so wrote Rabbeinu Yerucham; nevertheless for permission of benefit it appears to me as written, and see siman 467 notes 1 and 2. And Bach wrote that in any case we require that the wheat kernel softened, for if not there is no prohibition at all:",
  "chok-yaakov:9:ד":
    "Everything in any amount. And also the vessels used with them and the cooked dishes used with those vessels they also forbid, even though there are two any-amounts, as Magen Avraham wrote, unlike Taz as written above siman 467 note 5; also what Magen Avraham further challenged on Nehar Shalom I already wrote thus in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 39, and I brought his words briefly in siman 466 — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:10:א":
    "Permitted to sell it to a gentile. So Bach wrote in the name of Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah who wrote thus in the name of Mahari of Paris, and so in Maharil in the name of the Rosh; however Mordechai himself challenged this and forbids, and so in Agudah, and like the Rav's ruling in the gloss, and see above siman 467 notes 2 and 3 — all this specifically with a split wheat kernel as written in note 26:",
  "chok-yaakov:10:ב":
    "And if it was found on the eighth day. It implies even if the mixture occurred during Pesach (and even if he transgressed and retained intentionally as explained above siman 467) they are lenient in a mixture of any amount on the last day which is rabbinic to retain l'chatchila, and specifically if found on the eighth day, but if found during Pesach one must burn and destroy everything on chol ha-mo'ed, and if found on the seventh of Pesach see above beginning siman 466 how one should conduct — see there the implication of the Acharonim as written here:",
  "chok-yaakov:10:ג":
    "Permitted to retain it. Meaning in a mixture of any amount remove and it is permitted; but in something that is complete chametz forbidden d'oraisa, even on the last Yom Tov it is forbidden in any case rabbinically — so agreed the Acharonim, and it is plain:",
  "chok-yaakov:11:א":
    "And so they are accustomed during Pesach. Levush expanded to challenge the Rav on this, and his opinion is to permit in dough in every matter that is not taste imparted in cold, and Magen Avraham wrote in note 19 and this is its language: and I found written that the Gaon Maharal of Prague ruled to permit, but Maharash of Austria wrote to Rosh of Lublin to forbid, and so is the custom of most of the world, and in Krakow it appears to me it depends on custom — end quote. But in truth even though Levush reconciles the Tur's words not to forbid in cold, behold Mordechai forbids in meat salting even if a wheat kernel was found after salting — see there; and it is possible he forbade because there were cracks as written siman 465, and so Bach wrote here in the name of the Geonim; therefore he wrote regarding meat that has cracks it is forbidden by law in cold, and similarly in uncertainty whether it was salted from above it is also forbidden by law — end quote Magen Avraham. And still the obligation of explanation is not discharged, for since he forbids in meat because there are cracks, from this we say the wheat kernel imparts taste in any amount in cold and enters the cracks in the meat — if so all the more one should forbid in cold water or in dough more than cracks in meat; and this distinction is not applicable except regarding another solid lump like meat that has no cracks and is not a soft penetrating thing. And Taz concludes that even though for other cold one may permit like Levush, nevertheless in dough through handling in dough the wheat kernel is crushed and leavens and forbids the entire dough during Pesach if it softened (and similarly in chametz bread one should be stringent even in cold lest one be concerned for crumbs), and so the Rav's relative raised in his book — thus appears the conclusion of Magen Avraham and Bach and Maharshal. Nevertheless it appears to me according to what is written above note 18 that if the wheat kernel is not soaked one may permit in benefit b'eyna — if so all the more here in cold, for the opinion of many poskim is to permit in every matter; and it is possible the Rav's words and so they are accustomed also refer only to a soaked wheat kernel, as the Mechaber mentioned — if not he should have glossed here too that even if it is not split like in seif 9; rather it must be that since it is a cold thing his intent is not to be stringent at all if it is not soaked, since there is another side to be lenient; however Bach and other Acharonim who wrote one forbids only if the wheat kernel softened — from this they hold it is forbidden here even if it did not actually split open; and it is possible the Rav's gloss is also thus, and he did not gloss here because he relies on what he glossed in seif 9 — the same for all of them; nevertheless this is only to forbid in eating and not in benefit as written above note 26, and one who is lenient even in eating has not lost, for it is possible even if split one may be lenient in cold b'eyna, as the Rav wrote in the gloss and so they are accustomed — meaning one may be lenient b'eyna or in a time of distress, and so appears to me the essence:",
  "chok-yaakov:11:ב":
    "Here it was found and here it was. Magen Avraham wrote: if the wheat kernel is full of water and moist through and through, if so it appears it was in water, and in dough one should forbid the entire dough; nevertheless if there is room to attribute that it was swallowed from elsewhere from water and fell into the matzah, everything is permitted according to the matter, for we attribute in common cases whether to leniency or stringency, and it is not called an uncertainty at all — end quote, and see in my pamphlet of uncertainties; and he further wrote in Magen Avraham: that which we say here it was found is because we do not establish presumptive prohibition specifically from place to place or from piece to piece, but from time to time in one piece we establish presumptive prohibition (and so Peri Chadash in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 note 18), such as if he has dough in hand and cut from it and a piece of dough remained in his hand and a wheat kernel was found on it — all are forbidden, for we say perhaps it was previously (and also one cannot say it is double uncertainty that perhaps it did not leaven because it is uncertainty of lack of knowledge, and so agreed Taz in note 14) — see in responsum Mishna Berurah siman 69 and responsum Tzemach Tzedek siman 43. Responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 78 — incident: one took cabbage from barrels in a bowl and brought it elsewhere and a wheat kernel was found on it, and he permitted the cabbage in the barrels, for we say here it was found, here it was; but if afterward a wheat kernel was found in the barrels, even the cabbage in the bowl is forbidden, for we say perhaps the wheat kernel was previously in the barrels — end quote:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:א":
    "If they scalded the chicken. Rokeach siman 291 and so in Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah and this is its language: an incident occurred that they scalded a chicken in its mouth with its feathers in boiling water in a chametz second vessel, and they forbade eating it as we say in chapter Dam Chatat one pours boiling into it — end quote; therefore Taz wrote here that even in a second vessel it is forbidden, and this is also the Rav's opinion above siman 466 seif 3 in the gloss where he wrote two views regarding a second vessel on Pesach — see there. However in my humble opinion there is no dispute at all, for from that he brought proof from one pours boiling into boiling it implies it deals by law of pouring, and nevertheless it is called a second vessel regarding the bowl, as also written above siman 461 note 8 and so is the implication and meaning of the great poskim in their language — see there, and so is compelled, for from their words it implies we are not stringent because of Pesach stringency, for they learned from chapter Dam Chatat and Rokeach siman 431 ruled regarding other prohibitions to permit in a second vessel — rather it certainly deals by law of pouring; nevertheless for practical halachah one should be stringent as Rama decided above siman 467 that one should be stringent if the hand recoils in chametz, for the opinion of some poskim is to forbid even in cold; also I wrote above siman 461 note 8 that the opinion of some poskim is to forbid in a second vessel; also the words of Rokeach and Mordechai can be reconciled with strain that it deals with a second vessel — therefore one may not be lenient at all as stated:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ב":
    "Soaked wheat kernel. It is possible since the Rav did not gloss here as below seif 9 to forbid even if not soaked, because here since there is another uncertainty that perhaps afterward it fell we are not so stringent; however Taz wrote the Rav follows his view to forbid even if not soaked, only whatever softened — permitted to sell — end quote, and see above notes 18 and 22 — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ג":
    "The chicken is forbidden. Agreement of Rishonim and Acharonim that we do not say regarding a chicken here it was found here it was, and now it fell; and Magen Avraham wrote to distinguish that we do not say it was born now where there is no presumption of permission previously as stated in chapter 1 of Chullin and Niddah, and so Taz — on the contrary all impurities as at the time they are found (and see responsum Mishna Berurah and Tzemach Tzedek that I hinted above note 32); and even for Beit Shammai in chapter 1 of Niddah one may be stringent here since there are two adverse factors — wheats in the body and wheats that fell from outside, as the Talmud answers there page 3a regarding a rodent found in an alley — see there, and see Rambam chapter 15 and chapter 18 of Laws of Impurity, and see in my book Torat HaShelamim:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ד": "And there is one who forbids. See above note 31:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ה":
    "For we say perhaps afterward it fell there. Bach wrote and Rivash wrote in the name of Rashba: if a wheat kernel was found in a vessel in which they rinsed the chicken, we say here it was found here it was as with blemishes, and we do not say it came from the chicken — and so he ruled in practical halachah — end quote; Magen Avraham challenged this from Rashba's own words that he ruled if a wheat kernel was found in matzah they forbid the dough as written in note 11, and we do not say it fell now, and he expanded further to challenge this — many difficulties, and concludes it deals when they salted the chicken and afterward rinsed in a cold vessel, and afterward a wheat kernel was found in the vessel, and there is concern it was in the chicken and was salted with it — therefore we are not concerned for this since the wheat kernel was found in the vessel we do not say it was in the chicken because we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place; but in matzah which is from time to time as written — there is no dispute at all, unlike Rama's opinion — such is the content of his words there; even though his words at first glance are correct and he follows his view that from time to time aliba di-chol everyone establishes presumptive prohibition as he wrote in note 32; however in what he concluded that in matzah it is from time to time as written, and his intent to explain thus Rashba's opinion — this is astonishing, for on the contrary in matzah it is from place to place if not that it deals that dough remained in his hand as written in note 32 in his name, and this is against Rashba's language and implication, and the Rav's relative wrote in the name of Darkei Moshe many supports for this view — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ו": "From the force of double uncertainty. See above note 10:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ז":
    "Or in a whole wheat kernel. Tur wrote here, and the Rosh agreed that we do not derive prohibition from impurity, and it is like digested; and in Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 he wrote: pure that swallowed an impure fish is forbidden, for it is not considered digested since it is whole — from this Tur holds that when it is not whole he holds it is like digested; and that which he wrote here we do not derive prohibition from impurity that it is like digested is specifically when it is whole before us, unlike when it is broken we see it was digested before us — for presumably when the chicken ate it the wheat was whole, as Agudah chapter 1 of Bechoros, and so Shach explained Yoreh De'ah there the Tur's words (unlike some Acharonim there who explained the Tur's words per R' Meir of Pontise) — and this explanation is correct and essential, and so is clear from Tosafos' rulings chapter 1 of Bechoros, even though they seized in plainness that we do not learn prohibition from impurity for digestion — nevertheless Tosafos wrote there briefly that one forbids only when a whole wheat kernel is found, and it is found he ruled well here to be lenient through digestion when the wheat kernel is not whole — see there in Peri Chadash:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ח":
    "And chickens were salted there. Magen Avraham wrote: not specifically chickens but the same applies to meat, for here it deals with a whole wheat kernel where there is no digestion — end quote. And in Olat Shabbat he wrote: for amplification he cited chickens even though wheat kernels are commonly found in them — nevertheless no uncertainty emerges and we say perhaps afterward it fell; there is also another uncertainty that perhaps he holds like those who say it is not taste imparted at all in cold, which is like double uncertainty; Bach is stringent in these laws, and challenged the Rav — and the essence is like the Rav's words and so agreed the Rav's relative in his book and Taz in particular if the wheat kernel is not split open — plainly one should not be stringent as written above note 31, and without softening the wheat kernel one may be lenient in every matter, and so agreed Bach and the Acharonim:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:ט":
    "In uncertainties of rabbinic prohibition. See Bach what he wrote on this, and see what is written nearby note 40; and Nekudot HaKesef wrote chapter Orlah and he is Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 111 regarding two pots one of permission and one of prohibition, and before him two pieces one of permission and one of prohibition and this fell into that — if the prohibition is rabbinic it is permitted, for we say I say permission fell into permission, even if the permission in the pot is not majority — on this Nekudot HaKesef wrote: it is possible that for stringent rabbinic any-amount prohibitions we do not say I say, even in rabbinic prohibitions in less than majority — end quote; and Peri Chadash there challenged at the beginning of siman 111 without proof and wrote: since rabbinic any-amount prohibition we follow leniency in every uncertainty like all rabbinic uncertainty, and his words are not compelled to be lenient against Nekudot HaKesef's opinion, especially since we find many stringencies they were stringent on Pesach even in mixtures of any amount even in uncertainty; and Magen Avraham wrote: one who took into his mouth a piece of matzah and a piece of chicken and ate both together — one may permit the chicken or meat, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place, and we say it fell now in cold; and similarly the matzah one may permit; but if the meat rests in a bowl and he ate the meat from it, it is forbidden, for from time to time we establish presumptive prohibition, for perhaps it was in the bowl when he poured the meat into it (nevertheless according to majority it is recognizable whether the wheat kernel was cooked or baked with it or not) — however if they shook the bowl from place to place when it cooled we attribute to leniency, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place — end quote:",
  "chok-yaakov:12:י":
    "That was rinsed in water. Magen Avraham wrote: explanation — even though the wheat kernel was found inside while the meat was still soaking, nevertheless it is a rabbinic uncertainty, for there are those who permit in cold, and his words are not compelled to explain the Rav's opinion — see note 38; or scalded — meaning and afterward the wheat kernel was found, for one can say it fell now. And that which Rama ruled above seif 2 that double uncertainty does not help — that is for eating the wheats themselves, but the mixtures are permitted. Bach wrote in one uncertainty even to retain it is forbidden, even b'eyna, and in the end permitted to retain, and all the more permitted to sell them to a gentile and not for eating, for that uncertainty that perhaps it was digested is not a full uncertainty, for one can say digestion does not help in prohibition as written; and specifically complete chametz such as bread or dough, but a wheat kernel found in water permits the cooked dish cooked in it beforehand, for it is double uncertainty that perhaps it fell now, and if you say it fell before, perhaps it did not soften until now, and without softening there is no prohibition at all even according to our custom — end quote Magen Avraham:",
  "chok-yaakov:13:א":
    "Day-old. But if it is not day-old, even the hag'elah water itself is permitted on Pesach, since they hag'eled before Pesach and they are not day-old as explained above siman 461, and even fat that one melted before Pesach in a chametz vessel that is not day-old is permitted on Pesach as ruled in siman 467 seif 5 in the gloss, even though the Rav decided above that remove is forbidden on Pesach — all this is plain, and Bach toiled in vain on this — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:13:ב":
    "During Pesach. Bach wrote that even if they became mixed before Pesach in a matter nullified in sixty (even though it is also taste imparted by taste imparted of permission) nevertheless one may not use that water l'chatchila since one can easily find other water easily, and it is not for a cooked dish which is b'dieved, and if we forbid eating it on Pesach — end quote; Magen Avraham did not cite his language accurately; Taz wrote that sometimes even b'dieved it is forbidden in such a case if the sour waters remained above and did not mix, for they settled and stood and are as if in their own form unless he drew from it beforehand several times that through this it mixes — end quote. In my humble opinion one should not forbid b'dieved even though it was not nullified, nevertheless since it occurred before Pesach it is taste imparted by taste imparted of permission, and one should not forbid b'dieved — see siman 467 in the gloss and what is written there and siman 461 and 462 — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:13:ג":
    "Because they became mixed. They become one lump — if so even for those who permit in cold here it is forbidden since the body of leavening became mixed; and if a wheat kernel or other chametz is found in this pit, it depends on the dispute the Mechaber wrote above seif 12 whether he forbids in cold during Pesach — see above note 31; and see Taz, Beit Yosef, Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah, Rokeach siman 286, and Shulchan Lechem siman 63 who forbid if chametz is found in a well — and it appears it deals in a matter where there is concern for crumbs as written above; and so in responsum Rashba siman 176 — see there; and Bach wrote in this end in the name of Maharil that one should be careful for one who used on Pesach wells of gentiles or other wells where they are not careful all year that one should strain the water in a clean garment each time he draws — end quote; and see above siman 467 from the law of straining regarding vinegar and wine, and above siman 455 from the law of drawing water:",
  "chok-yaakov:14:א":
    "Rinsing suffices. In general in cold without gravy, all agree it does not forbid — end quote Magen Avraham; even though in a chicken it is impossible without a little moisture, unlike in cold it does not absorb according to everyone except through a little salting or through handling in dough and such; Responsum Tzemach Tzedek: question — if a little beer remained in a vessel and he drew water into it and rinsed other meat after salting on Pesach — it is close to absorption and has the law of a wheat kernel salted with the chicken for each opinion in this seif. I already challenged him in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 6 note 23, for water nullifies the power of salt and it has only the law of cold and is not forbidden through rinsing alone, even if there are cracks, for the Rav ruled above siman 465 to forbid in goose — specifically there where he rubs well in its crop or scalds inside as written above note 31 in the name of Mordechai, and see above siman 465 note 6 — unlike mere rinsing (and with this one can also explain what Bach challenged here on the Rosh — see there); and mixture of any amount — nevertheless because of Pesach stringency I raised there one should peel a little of the meat he rinsed, and it is a stringency in itself:",
  "chok-yaakov:14:ב":
    "After salting. Magen Avraham wrote: explanation — while still in its salt; but if found after it was salted and rinsed, we say it fell now, for if you say it was on it beforehand it would have fallen during rinsing when he rinsed, and specifically when found on the chicken, but if found inside the chicken it was certainly previously — see Niddah page 3 regarding a box that has rims — end quote; and see siman 467 note 6 where I wrote pure salted and impure bland is also permitted here, and it deals when the wheat kernel was also salted with the chicken — see there; and I saw meticulous people are careful on Pesach before scalding and salting to open the chicken well or cut it in two and check well that no wheat kernel is found inside, and Knesset HaGedolah wrote one who does so is zealous and rewarded — end quote:",
  "chok-yaakov:14:ג":
    "And to permit the rest through peeling. And if he transgressed and cooked without peeling, see siman 467 note 9; and in Magen Avraham he wrote here various distinctions in this law, and the essence as written there; and regarding the essential law many Acharonim in their responsa, for some ruled to burn everything and some Acharonim permit the rest of the pieces entirely, and so the Rav himself ruled in responsum siman 108; and the essence appears to me like Taz's conclusion and this is its language: and in my humble opinion for practical conduct one should act thus: if a wheat kernel is found on salted meat dry without gravy, one may permit retaining everything, only where it was found one peels, and the rest may be retained until after Pesach; but if the wheat kernel is found in the gravy, then it is considered like cooked, for in a slight measure it is like cooked in gravy — then everything resting in the gravy should be forbidden, but what rests above the gravy one may retain until after Pesach or do as Rama wrote at the end to sell to a gentile what is not in the gravy — end quote; and his words appear correct, and all the more if the wheat kernel is not split open one should not be stringent in salting at all as written above note 26; and it is possible the Rav's gloss too is only stringent with a split wheat kernel that the Mechaber mentioned, even though Taz follows his view that it deals even if not split open — see notes 26 and 31, and see responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 91 where his opinion also appears to be lenient regarding salting even during Pesach — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:15:א":
    "Split wheat kernel. Knesset HaGedolah wrote: since the Rav did not gloss here too even if not split open, it appears he holds if not split open one forbids only in roasting as much as removal — end quote; however the opinion of other Acharonim is not so — see above note 26 and Machatzit HaShekel:",
  "chok-yaakov:15:ב":
    "Place of bubbling. It deals with a thin chicken, for if not it bubbles and the whole thing is forbidden as explained above siman 467 — see there:",
  "chok-yaakov:15:ג":
    "According to how they turn the spit. See in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 28 note 2 what I challenged on the separation, and I wrote specifically for rabbinic any-amount prohibition we are concerned for this, unlike other prohibitions — see there; and I saw Magen Avraham also aligned with this distinction literally as written there:",
  "chok-yaakov:15:ד":
    "When they turn the spit. The Rav's relative wrote in his book: if the spit is not turned, it appears from Rama's opinion that the law of roasting is like the law of salting — end quote:",
  "chok-yaakov:15:ה":
    "Touching one another. Magen Avraham wrote: if the chicken is fatty, even if they do not touch, it forbids everything — end quote; nevertheless when not split open it appears to me one should not be stringent as written above:",
  "chok-yaakov:16:_":
    "The bird is permitted. And even peeling is not needed, for the removal is in the place of peeling; Bach ruled to forbid the whole thing, and the Rav's relative decides to forbid in eating and permit in benefit, and other Acharonim agree to permit entirely in scalding:",
};

